\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Induced Euclidean volume on a subspace}
\date{2026-10-25}

\p{Let #{H} be a finite-dimensional real inner-product space, in
particular a subspace of #{(X_V,h_V)} with its induced topology and
inner product. Choose an orthonormal chart #{C:H\to\mathbb R^k}.
The \newvocab{induced Euclidean measure} is
#{\lambda_H=(C^{-1})_*\lambda_{\{1,\ldots,k\}}} on its Borel sets.
It is independent of the orthonormal chart. If #{H=\{0\}}, it is
unit mass at zero. For an affine translate #{a+H}, use the pushforward
of #{\lambda_H} under #{h\mapsto a+h}.}
\proof{
\p{A finite Gram-Schmidt construction produces an orthonormal basis:
normalize a nonzero vector by its positive norm, subtract its components
from a spanning family, and continue in the orthogonal complement.
Positivity permits normalization at each nonzero step. Two such charts
differ by a linear isometric isomorphism of #{\mathbb R^k};
\ref{fdip-002L} proves independence. A different choice of origin
in #{a+H} differs by a translation in #{H}, which preserves its
coordinate measure by \ref{fdip-002K}.}
\p{For a proper linear subspace #{H\subsetneq X_V}, extend its
orthonormal basis to one of #{X_V} by the same construction. In those
coordinates #{H} lies in a coordinate hyperplane and is closed.
Within each bounded cube that hyperplane has measure zero: the
rectangle formula has a zero-measure singleton factor and finite other
factors. Monotonicity and countable exhaustion give zero ambient
measure; orthogonal invariance transports this back. But the unit
coordinate cube in #{H} has induced mass one. Thus induced volume is
not the restriction of ambient #{\lambda_V}.}
}
\p{For example, on #{H=\{(s,s):s\in\mathbb R\}}, the segment
#{0\le s\le1} has induced length #{\sqrt2}, ambient area zero,
and raw #{s}-coordinate length one. Indeed its unit vector is
#{(1,1)/\sqrt2}, so its orthonormal coordinate runs from zero to
#{\sqrt2}. An arbitrary basis therefore needs its volume factor.}
\p{The argument uses the transformation convention of
\citet{Exercise 1.2.21, p. 41, preliminary version}{tao2011measure}.
Mathlib's \leanref{OrthonormalBasis.measurePreserving_repr} targets
EuclideanSpace; \leanref{PiLp.volume_preserving_ofLp} and
\leanref{PiLp.volume_preserving_toLp} identify its real finite PiLp
volume with ordinary coordinate products. TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{Module.Basis.volume_eq_smul_addHaar} records the
square-root Gram determinant factor for an arbitrary basis, while
\leanref/tauceti{AffineIsometryEquiv.measurePreserving} applies to
finite-dimensional real Borel inner-product spaces. These compare the
same canonical-volume convention.}
